    (v_topic_id, 'A 37-old man presents with flaccid, fluid-filled blisters on the trunk and painful oral erosions. A punch biopsy for direct immunofluorescence (DIF) is performed on perilesional skin. What immunofluorescence pattern is diagnostic for this patient''s disease?', '["Intercellular IgG and C3 deposition in a ''fishnet'' or reticular pattern throughout the epidermis", "Granular IgA deposition concentrated at the tips of the dermal papillae", "Linear IgA deposition along the dermo-epidermal junction", "Linear band of IgG and C3 deposition along the basement membrane zone", "Shaggy deposition of fibrinogen at the dermo-epidermal junction without immunoglobulins"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Direct immunofluorescence (DIF) of perilesional skin in pemphigus vulgaris reveals intercellular deposition of IgG and C3 throughout the epidermis in a net-like, reticular, or ''fishnet'' pattern, reflecting autoantibodies bound to desmogleins in the desmosomes.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 73-old man with severe pemphigus vulgaris is hospitalized due to widespread cutaneous denudation and severe pain. Which of the following immunotherapies is first-line, gold-standard therapy to induce rapid remission in combination with systemic corticosteroids?', '["Oral Doxycycline and Nicotinamide", "Intravenous Cyclosporine", "Dapsone therapy", "Strict gluten-free diet", "Rituximab (anti-CD20 monoclonal antibody)"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Rituximab, a chimeric monoclonal antibody targeting CD20 on B-lymphocytes, is now established as first-line therapy (in combination with a tapering course of systemic corticosteroids) for moderate-to-severe pemphigus vulgaris. It leads to rapid depletion of antibody-producing B-cell clones, achieving higher and faster remission rates compared to corticosteroids alone.'),
