    (v_topic_id, 'A 67-old lady with Dermatitis Herpetiformis is prescribed Dapsone, which leads to rapid improvement in their severe skin itching. Which of the following is the most critical screening step required PRIOR to initiating Dapsone therapy?', '["Serum IgA tissue transglutaminase (tTG) level", "Thiopurine methyltransferase (TPMT) activity", "Glucose-6-Phosphate Dehydrogenase (G6PD) activity level", "Human Immunodeficiency Virus (HIV) serology", "HLA-B*5701 genetic testing"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Before starting Dapsone, it is mandatory to screen patients for Glucose-6-Phosphate Dehydrogenase (G6PD) deficiency. Dapsone causes oxidative stress on red blood cells, which can trigger severe hemolytic anemia and methemoglobinemia in G6PD-deficient individuals.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 69-old male presenting with symmetric, intensely itchy grouped vesicles on their elbows is diagnosed with Dermatitis Herpetiformis. While Dapsone successfully controls their skin symptoms, what long-term therapy is essential to prevent bowel complications and resolve the disease completely?', '["Strict lifelong gluten-free diet", "Chronic treatment with Rituximab", "Avoidance of thiol-containing foods", "Long-term oral Prednisone therapy", "Avoidance of sun exposure and hot baths"]'::jsonb, 0, 'While Dapsone is highly effective for rapidly clearing the pruritic cutaneous lesions of Dermatitis Herpetiformis, it does not treat the underlying enteropathy. A strict lifelong gluten-free diet is the essential, disease-modifying therapy. It eventually allows Dapsone to be discontinued and protects against intestinal lymphoma risk.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 23-old female hospitalized for osteomyelitis is treated with intravenous Vancomycin. On day 8 of therapy, they develop a sudden eruption of tense vesicles and bullae arranged in an annular, rosette-like configuration (''crown of jewels'' or ''string of pearls'') on the trunk and thighs. Direct immunofluorescence (DIF) shows linear IgA deposition along the basement membrane zone. What is the first-line management?', '["Apply high-potency topical Clobetasol cream and continue Vancomycin", "Initiate urgent intravenous Rituximab", "Initiate high-dose systemic Prednisone while continuing Vancomycin", "Administer high-dose Intravenous Immunoglobulin (IVIG)", "Discontinue Vancomycin immediately and start Dapsone if needed"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Linear IgA Bullous Dermatosis (LABD) can be idiopathic or drug-induced, with Vancomycin being the classic and most common drug trigger. Drug-induced LABD classically presents with a ''string of pearls'' or ''crown of jewels'' arrangement of vesicles. The most critical first-line action is immediate discontinuation of the offending drug (Vancomycin), which often leads to complete clearing of lesions.'),
