    (v_topic_id, 'A 55-old gentleman presents with flaccid, fluid-filled blisters on the trunk and painful oral erosions. A punch biopsy for direct immunofluorescence (DIF) is performed on perilesional skin. What immunofluorescence pattern is diagnostic for this patient''s disease?', '["Intercellular IgG and C3 deposition in a ''fishnet'' or reticular pattern throughout the epidermis", "Linear band of IgG and C3 deposition along the basement membrane zone", "Shaggy deposition of fibrinogen at the dermo-epidermal junction without immunoglobulins", "Granular IgA deposition concentrated at the tips of the dermal papillae", "Linear IgA deposition along the dermo-epidermal junction"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Direct immunofluorescence (DIF) of perilesional skin in pemphigus vulgaris reveals intercellular deposition of IgG and C3 throughout the epidermis in a net-like, reticular, or ''fishnet'' pattern, reflecting autoantibodies bound to desmogleins in the desmosomes.'),
