    (v_topic_id, 'A 48-old man is evaluated for multiple flaccid vesicles and weeping erosions on the scalp, trunk, and groin. Stroking apparently normal skin adjacent to an active lesion causes easy sloughing of the epidermis. A punch biopsy of an active lesion is performed. What is the classic histologic finding expected on hematoxylin and eosin (H&E) staining?', '["Interface dermatitis with vacuolar degeneration of the basal layer and colloid bodies", "Subepidermal clefting with neutrophilic microabscesses at the dermal papillary tips", "Subcorneal acantholysis with cleavage within the granular layer", "Subepidermal clefting with numerous eosinophils in the blister cavity", "Intraepidermal acantholysis with a ''tombstone'' appearance of the basal layer"]'::jsonb, 4, 'The clinical sign described (easy sloughing of epidermis with friction) is Nikolsky''s sign, classic for pemphigus. Histopathology of pemphigus vulgaris demonstrates suprabasal intraepidermal acantholysis (loss of cohesion between keratinocytes). The basal layer remains attached to the basement membrane zone via intact hemidesmosomes, presenting as a single layer of basal cells lining the floor of the cavity, known as a ''tombstone'' appearance.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 28-old female presents with flaccid, fluid-filled blisters on the trunk and painful oral erosions. A punch biopsy for direct immunofluorescence (DIF) is performed on perilesional skin. What immunofluorescence pattern is diagnostic for this patient''s disease?', '["Granular IgA deposition concentrated at the tips of the dermal papillae", "Shaggy deposition of fibrinogen at the dermo-epidermal junction without immunoglobulins", "Intercellular IgG and C3 deposition in a ''fishnet'' or reticular pattern throughout the epidermis", "Linear band of IgG and C3 deposition along the basement membrane zone", "Linear IgA deposition along the dermo-epidermal junction"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Direct immunofluorescence (DIF) of perilesional skin in pemphigus vulgaris reveals intercellular deposition of IgG and C3 throughout the epidermis in a net-like, reticular, or ''fishnet'' pattern, reflecting autoantibodies bound to desmogleins in the desmosomes.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 82-old woman with severe pemphigus vulgaris is hospitalized due to widespread cutaneous denudation and severe pain. Which of the following immunotherapies is first-line, gold-standard therapy to induce rapid remission in combination with systemic corticosteroids?', '["Intravenous Cyclosporine", "Oral Doxycycline and Nicotinamide", "Strict gluten-free diet", "Rituximab (anti-CD20 monoclonal antibody)", "Dapsone therapy"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Rituximab, a chimeric monoclonal antibody targeting CD20 on B-lymphocytes, is now established as first-line therapy (in combination with a tapering course of systemic corticosteroids) for moderate-to-severe pemphigus vulgaris. It leads to rapid depletion of antibody-producing B-cell clones, achieving higher and faster remission rates compared to corticosteroids alone.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 43-old gentleman presents with a 6-month history of superficial, crusty, and scaly plaques on their face, scalp, and upper back in a ''seborrheic'' distribution. The patient reports that these lesions originally began as fragile, fluid-filled blisters that ruptured immediately. Mucous membranes are completely spared. What is the autoantigen targeted in this condition?', '["BP180 (Type XVII Collagen)", "Laminin-332 (epiligrin)", "Desmoplakin and Envoplakin", "Desmoglein 3 only", "Desmoglein 1 only"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Pemphigus foliaceus (PF) is a superficial variant of pemphigus. Because the target autoantigen is Desmoglein 1 (which is only expressed in the superficial epidermis and is not present in mucous membranes), patients present with superficial, flaccid blisters that rupture easily, leaving hyperkeratotic, crusty plaques resembling ''cornflakes.'' Mucosal involvement is characteristically absent because Desmoglein 3 compensates in mucosal tissue.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A patient from rural Brazil presents with multiple crusted, erythematous plaques on their face and chest, accompanied by a severe burning sensation. Widespread endemics of this condition occur in certain regions of Brazil, where it is known as ''fogo selvagem''. What is the primary environmental trigger or vector suspected in its pathogenesis?', '["Chronic exposure to ultraviolet B (UVB) radiation", "Reactivation of latent Varicella Zoster Virus", "Infestation by Sarcoptes scabiei mites", "Bites from the black fly (Simulium nigrimanum)", "Ingestion of high-gluten grain products"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Fogo selvagem is an endemic form of pemphigus foliaceus found in rural parts of Brazil (particularly near rivers). Epidemiological evidence strongly links it to environmental triggers, specifically autoantibody cross-reactivity initiated by salivary antigens from the black fly (Simulium nigrimanum) vector.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 82-old gentleman presents with multiple superficial, oozing, and crusted erosions on their chest and upper back. A punch biopsy of an active lesion is performed. H&E histopathology reveals very superficial intraepidermal acantholysis with cleavage localized to the subcorneal or granular layer. What is the diagnosis?', '["Bullous Pemphigoid", "Pemphigus Vulgaris", "Dermatitis Herpetiformis", "Pemphigus Foliaceus", "Epidermolysis Bullosa Acquisita"]'::jsonb, 3, 'In pemphigus foliaceus, acantholysis occurs superficially, classically in the subcorneal or granular layer of the epidermis. This is because Desmoglein 1 (the target of the autoantibodies) is concentrated in the superficial layers of the epidermis, leading to very superficial blister roof breakdown.');
